│  └──────┘                                                                          │
│  ┌──────┐ 3  Excel — paste into "status.csv", reformat dates  9:16:40              │
│  │▦▦▦▦▦ │    ↳ date format: 2026-10-21 → Oct 21                                    │
│  └──────┘                                                                          │
│     ⋮   8 more steps                                     [ Show all ]  [ Edit steps ]│
│                                                                                    │
│  WHAT WE'D BUILD  (proposal — you can change it before building)                   │
│  A Python script, run from the menu bar or every Tuesday 9:00, that pulls your     │
│  sprint tickets from the Jira API, writes status.csv, and drafts (doesn't send)    │
│  the email in Mail.                                                                │
│  Will need: Jira API token · write access to ~/Documents/Reports · Mail drafts     │
│  Estimated build cost: ~$0.30–$1.00 of Meta API usage                              │
│                                                                                    │
│  [ Not useful ▾ ]   [ Snooze ▾ ]   ⋯  Never suggest for Jira                       │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

Design notes:

- **Title is a verb phrase in the user's words**, generated from the steps. Never "Workflow #12".
- **Time saved shows its arithmetic.** An unexplained "saves 1.6 h" reads as marketing, and a
  visible formula reads as a measurement. If the estimate is shaky, the confidence line says why.
- **Confidence is a word plus a reason**, never a percentage. "73%" looks precise but tells the
  user nothing they can act on.
- **Evidence thumbnails** are redacted crops around the active region (not full screens), with
  step text beside them. Clicking a thumbnail opens it large, in the Activity timeline context.
  "Session ▾" switches between the 7 observed occurrences, which is how users check that it
  really is the same task.
- **"Edit steps"** lets the user correct the pattern (merge, delete, mark "this varies") before
  building. Correcting before building is much cheaper than repairing afterwards.
- **"Not useful ▾"** opens a one-click reason list: *Already automated* · *Not actually
  repetitive* · *Too rare to matter* · *I want to do this myself* · *Steps are wrong* ·
  *Other…*. The reason feeds the pattern engine (track 09) and the item disappears with a 5 s
  Undo.
- **"Never suggest for Jira"** is deliberately a text-style button behind the overflow-adjacent
  position. It's permanent and broad, so it shouldn't be hit by accident, but it's still one
  click. It adds a rule visible in Privacy › Exclusions with an Undo toast.

### 6.3 The build brief (the step between "Automate this" and Muse)

Clicking **Automate this** opens a sheet, not the builder. The sheet is the spec Muse will
receive, written in plain language and editable, plus **exactly what will be sent to Meta**.

```
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  Build: Weekly status CSV                                                │
│                                                                          │
│  Goal      Pull my sprint tickets and produce status.csv + an email draft│
│  Trigger   ( ) Manually  (•) Every Tuesday 9:00  ( ) When I press ⌃⌥⌘S    │
│  Must      ☑ Draft emails, never send   ☑ Only write in ~/Documents/Reports│
│  Notes     [ "pick the oldest ticket first" (from Teach)               ] │
│                                                                          │
│  Sent to Meta for this build:  11 step descriptions (text, redacted)     │
│                                3 redacted screenshots    [ Review… ]     │
│  ☐ Send step text only (no screenshots) — the build may be less accurate │
│                                                                          │
│  Budget for this build  [ $2.00 ▾ ]        [ Cancel ]  [ Start building ]│
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

The **"Must"** constraints become hard constraints in the Muse prompt *and* become the
automation's declared permissions (§8), so the user writes the safety rules once.

