plan/research/09-pattern-engine.md:352:## 9. Evaluation and feedback
plan/research/09-pattern-engine.md:354:[assumption] Build a consented, local corpus by recording the developer doing 12 workflow families with eight executions each across six weeks: spreadsheet-to-form transfer, download/rename/import, weekly report, expense draft, file conversion, and native-app equivalents. Include exceptions, interruptions, IME typing, permission denial, and unrelated browsing. Add at least 100 negative episodes: research, creative writing, navigation, and coincidental copy/paste. Use sandbox accounts and fabricated business data. Hand-label episode boundaries, task identity, essential steps, transfers, correct automation scope, and reasonable savings. A second reviewer checks 20%; owner-only labels are useful development evidence, not a product-quality estimate.
plan/research/09-pattern-engine.md:368:[assumption] Begin with explicit rules and a Beta(1,1) acceptance prior by pattern category, using only resolved responses. Do not treat ignores as dismissals. Calibrate acceptance probabilities later with user-grouped validation; until then expose evidence-quality components rather than a spurious “92% confidence.” Hold out a small, budget-limited shadow candidate set for offline review to detect ranking blind spots without increasing notification volume.
plan/research/09-pattern-engine.md:370:## 10. Product-killing risks and implementation order
plan/research/09-pattern-engine.md:374:[assumption] Implement in order: (1) schemas, privacy gate, encrypted replay store, and teach-session fixture recording; (2) deterministic actions, boundaries, and n-gram mining with evidence viewer; (3) capability registry and ranked cards; (4) real-key Meta transport and schema-validated explanations; (5) constrained PrefixSpan and vector neighbors only when held-out recall justifies them. This keeps a solo developer's first milestone testable without an open-ended multimodal agent.
