plan/research/05-alt-builders.md:156:## API-key path: explicit for every Meta route
plan/research/05-alt-builders.md:167:| Direct loop / DSL LLM node | Native HTTP client sends `Authorization: Bearer <key>` to approved Meta origin; no key in graph or WebView |
plan/research/05-alt-builders.md:173:## The Macroscope SDK/DSL contract
plan/research/05-alt-builders.md:175:### A graph with typed effects, not JavaScript disguised as data
plan/research/05-alt-builders.md:181:**[assumption]** Generate TypeScript authoring bindings for developers, but compile into this graph before installation. TypeScript is an authoring convenience, not a security boundary. New native primitives require app development and review. Advanced arbitrary code is visibly classified separately and executed in a sandboxed worker without unrestricted IPC or direct access to desktop privileges.
plan/research/05-alt-builders.md:183:### Primitives and permissions
plan/research/05-alt-builders.md:198:| `review.effect(preview) -> ApprovalToken` | Host UI binds approval to artifact hash, resolved target and payload hash; cannot be forged by a node |
plan/research/05-alt-builders.md:206:### Triggers, parameters, lifecycle
plan/research/05-alt-builders.md:212:**[assumption]** Static validation checks types, graph bounds, referenced outputs and the union of required effects. A dry run replays fixtures and stubs external effects; it cannot prove that a live website still matches. The first real run previews file differences, destination and submitted fields. Each run journals node outcomes and idempotency keys. Recovery resumes after completed safe nodes, but checks uncertain remote mutations before retrying. File writes can have reversible backups; remote sends and UI submissions cannot promise universal undo.
plan/research/05-alt-builders.md:214:## Three example automations
plan/research/05-alt-builders.md:218:### A. Capture a copied expense line into a workbook
plan/research/05-alt-builders.md:242:**[assumption]** Permissions: clipboard read, read/write the selected table, notifications. No network or LLM egress. Blank/ambiguous dates fail; amounts parse as decimal money rather than floating-point arithmetic. Duplicate hotkeys produce an upsert preview rather than duplicate rows. Spreadsheet adapters write merchant strings as literal cells, including values beginning with `=`. Fixtures include duplicates, invalid currency amounts and formula-looking merchant names.
plan/research/05-alt-builders.md:244:### B. Tuesday issue report to CSV and a draft summary
plan/research/05-alt-builders.md:262:    files.writeAtomic("csv", reports, runDatedName("status", ".csv"),
plan/research/05-alt-builders.md:273:### C. Fill a desktop form from a selected CSV row
plan/research/05-alt-builders.md:278:  params: { input: fileParam(".csv"), app: appParam(), row: integerParam(1, 1000) },
plan/research/05-alt-builders.md:280:    files.read("csv", param("input"), { maxBytes: 1000000 }),
plan/research/05-alt-builders.md:281:    table.csvRow("client", out("csv"), param("row"), clientSchema),
plan/research/05-alt-builders.md:298:## Delivery sequence, fallbacks and product-killing risks
plan/research/05-alt-builders.md:300:**[assumption]** First build the graph validator, broker and replay fixtures for clipboard, CSV and files. Add direct Meta generation and one repair pass. Then prototype full-code builds with exec in disposable projects; graduate interactive builds to MSP once initialization, approvals, recovery and cancellation pass. Add terminal access only after the native result flow works. Delay alternative-provider adapters and broad desktop control until demonstrated demand.
plan/research/05-alt-builders.md:306:- **Prompt injection/privacy:** captured screen text, web content and tool outputs are data. The generator cannot expand the permission ceiling. Separate consent covers cloud egress; observation permission does not authorize automation. Test attacks through CSV cells, UI labels and issue descriptions.
plan/research/05-alt-builders.md:307:- **Isolation:** workspaces/worktrees separate changes but are not security sandboxes. A broad coding worker must not inherit the capture archive, browser cookies, credentials or the privileged AX broker. Prove OS enforcement; stop if the host reports sandbox unavailable.
plan/research/05-alt-builders.md:311:- **Windows reality:** design backend and capability contracts now; require Windows CI plus hands-on external testing for PTY, filesystem paths, UI Automation and cancellation. **[assumption]** Unsupported Windows capabilities should be explicit, rather than inferred from Mac success.
plan/research/05-alt-builders.md:313:## Open questions for the owner
plan/research/05-alt-builders.md:315:- Is advanced arbitrary-code automation part of the first public release, or can v1 ship typed workflows with Muse available in a developer preview?
plan/research/05-alt-builders.md:320:## Decisions I made
plan/research/05-alt-builders.md:322:- Make the typed Macroscope graph the default saved artifact and runtime contract.
plan/research/14-ux-codex.md:1:# Macroscope, summoned: teach a task, inspect its story, keep a tool
plan/research/14-ux-codex.md:7:## 1. The decision: an instrument drawer, not an operations center
plan/research/14-ux-codex.md:9:Make Macroscope a small window you summon when you want help, with three verbs: **Run, Teach, Review**. Its enduring objects are named tools, such as “Prepare Tuesday status CSV,” rather than discoveries, conversations, or agents. Discovery works quietly in the background after explicit consent. Building happens in a temporary, resizable storyboard. A completed tool returns to the drawer.
plan/research/14-ux-codex.md:19:## 2. Observation is a choice, not the price of admission
plan/research/14-ux-codex.md:26:Neither route needs a Meta key until the first model request. Someone can explore, record, and edit a local draft before paying for generation. Explain at capture time that the demonstrated task runs against the user's real desktop: a recording is not a rehearsal or sandbox. Recommend a sample file or test account before recording a destructive workflow.
plan/research/14-ux-codex.md:36:## 3. Screen one: the summonable tool drawer
plan/research/14-ux-codex.md:48:| > Prepare Tuesday status CSV                         Enter |
plan/research/14-ux-codex.md:52:| PREPARE TUESDAY STATUS CSV                                 |
plan/research/14-ux-codex.md:53:| Input  [Choose export.csv...]                              |
plan/research/14-ux-codex.md:54:| Output A new CSV; the source stays untouched               |
plan/research/14-ux-codex.md:66:Selecting a tool never executes it. Enter runs only when the explicit Run control has focus, or after an equally explicit reviewed keyboard action. A result moving under the user's pointer cannot become an accidental execution. Avoid automatic clipboard inspection; “Use clipboard” is a deliberate input action with a preview.
plan/research/14-ux-codex.md:72:Permission chips use concrete nouns: “Read: selected CSV,” not “Filesystem access.” Never truncate the scope to a misleading summary. A chip opens details, expiry, revocation, and the distinction between build-time access and runtime access. The prominent label is “Ask before creating a calendar event,” not “trusted.”
plan/research/14-ux-codex.md:74:## 4. Discovery is an appointment, not a notification stream
plan/research/14-ux-codex.md:80:> “A similar Jira-to-spreadsheet sequence appeared on 4 workdays. This might be the same task. If each occurrence takes about 6 minutes, a tool could save part of that time. Suggested first version: prepare the CSV; leave sending to you.”
plan/research/14-ux-codex.md:90:## 5. Screen two: correct the storyboard before writing code
plan/research/14-ux-codex.md:96:| < Tools       Prepare Tuesday status CSV          Draft saved locally |
plan/research/14-ux-codex.md:99:| RESULT: [A CSV of open items, dates YYYY-MM-DD, owner included      ] |
plan/research/14-ux-codex.md:100:| INPUT:  [A CSV exported by me]       [Use a sample file]              |
plan/research/14-ux-codex.md:104:| 2 [Observed] Read six columns               [Approved frame preview] |
plan/research/14-ux-codex.md:106:| 4 [You said] Save a new output CSV          Date source: [created v]  |
plan/research/14-ux-codex.md:127:## 6. Build without making the transcript the product
plan/research/14-ux-codex.md:135:Keep model/provider configuration configurable; do not depend on an unverified endpoint or model capability. The brief reports `muse-wonder-1.0` used by a local test, but that is not a promise of future availability. **[local: `plan/BRIEF.md`]** No consumer login is part of onboarding. Do not depend on the unverified `muse serve` environment-auth path.
plan/research/14-ux-codex.md:141:Before a paid call, show the data packet and user-set spending limit. Dollar estimates require verified pricing and are otherwise omitted. Reserve budget before starting concurrent calls; reconcile actual usage afterward. If a strict provider-side dollar cap is unavailable, explain the limit's precision rather than promising exact enforcement. Cancelling stops further work where possible; it does not promise to erase charges for an in-flight request.
plan/research/14-ux-codex.md:143:## 7. Screen three: the proof bench and run contract
plan/research/14-ux-codex.md:145:“It compiled” is not the trust event. The user needs to see that a representative input produces the intended result, with the promised boundary. The proof bench combines a sample comparison with the proposed run contract.
plan/research/14-ux-codex.md:152:| 18 rows from sample-export.csv       18 rows in status-preview.csv   |
plan/research/14-ux-codex.md:163:| Reads: one CSV you select          Writes: one new CSV                |
plan/research/14-ux-codex.md:175:For file transformations, a test uses staged copies and a new output location. For actions involving real services, distinguish preview, genuine service sandbox, and live limited trial. If no sandbox exists, say so. Default to stopping at a prepared draft before sending, submitting, deleting, or paying. An “Undo” control appears only when a compensating action really exists; otherwise offer recovery instructions and an activity receipt.
plan/research/14-ux-codex.md:179:## 8. The complete loop, including failure
plan/research/14-ux-codex.md:214:**Build:** they correct “updated date” to “created date,” choose a new-file output policy, inspect the outbound request, add their key, and approve generation. The app preserves the story if the key is rejected or the quota is exhausted. Retrying never silently increases the approved budget.
plan/research/14-ux-codex.md:218:**Trust:** they save the tool with “Ask for a CSV each run.” Next Tuesday they select a file and run. A receipt links input identity, tool version, output location, actual permissions exercised, and any unverified conditions. It does not expose raw sensitive values in the launcher.
plan/research/14-ux-codex.md:220:**Repair:** a later export changes a column name. The tool stops before writing, explains “Expected created_date; found created,” and offers Choose another input, Correct mapping, or Prepare a repair. It never silently rewrites itself against live data. The repair packet previews the relevant schema and error; the user can redact it before another Meta call. After generation, test both the new sample and retained approved fixtures. Keep the previous version available, but never rerun a partially completed external action automatically. Unknown outcomes require reconciliation before retry.
plan/research/14-ux-codex.md:224:## 9. Where this beats the standard model, and where it loses
plan/research/14-ux-codex.md:230:| Decide what a tool can do | This proposal | A contract sits beside the action and is bound to a version. A dedicated privacy center still offers better global inspection. |
plan/research/14-ux-codex.md:237:The standard architecture can adopt story editing and contracts without becoming palette-first. Those two ideas are stronger than the claim that a small window is inherently better. My recommendation is therefore firm about the object model and cautious about window size: ship tools, stories, proofs, and grants; test whether the drawer actually improves access.
plan/research/14-ux-codex.md:239:## 10. Accessibility, privacy operations, and launch gates
plan/research/14-ux-codex.md:250:- **Cost:** ambient cloud analysis could consume budget before producing a useful tool. Start with local coarse discovery and paid calls at explicit boundaries. Measure total paid cost per retained useful tool, including unsuccessful builds and repairs.
plan/research/14-ux-codex.md:254:Prototype validation should use six target users as a directional study, not statistical proof. Give each the same file task, a mistaken inference, a permission expansion, and a partial failure. Compare against the standard flow. Proposed success criteria: at least five can explain the tool's scope before running; at least five correct the mistaken rule without help; nobody mistakes preview for live execution. Measure time to useful tested output, accidental execution, abandoned drafts, recall of outbound data, and repair success. A fast launcher that worsens those comprehension measures loses.
plan/research/14-ux-codex.md:256:## Open questions for the owner
plan/research/14-ux-codex.md:263:## Decisions I made
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:1:# Macroscope architecture: independent take A
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:7:## 1. Decision: Tauri 2, React, TypeScript, Rust, a small Swift capture helper
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:9:Choose **(a) Tauri 2**, with **React + TypeScript + Vite**, a Rust application core, and a signed Swift observer helper on macOS. Implement the Windows observer using Rust and Windows APIs. Do not build two native application interfaces, embed Flutter, or ship a permanent Python/Node service. This recommendation prioritizes low background overhead and one maintainable interface while accepting WebKit/WebView2 differences and some native integration work. **[assumption]**
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:11:Tauri's core manages windows and tray interfaces; its web content uses operating-system webviews, specifically WKWebView on macOS and WebView2 on Windows. It does not bundle those webview libraries. That establishes the structural opportunity to keep a small native resident core and destroy unused interface windows; it does **not** prove a particular RAM saving. [verified: https://v2.tauri.app/concept/process-model/] [verified: https://v2.tauri.app/reference/webview-versions/]
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:15:### Serious comparison
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:24:| Micro-app hosting | Existing web stack, separate unprivileged host windows | Consistent Chromium; strong embedding primitives | Desktop webview coverage becomes another dependency | Separate WKWebView/WebView2 integration |
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:40:## 2. User-visible architecture and performance contract
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:44:Tauri documents native tray construction and menu callbacks in Rust; implement those there so no webview is necessary for Pause or Quit. Create Dashboard, Builder, and each pinned tool as distinct logical windows. Keep ordinary title bars and standard keyboard shortcuts initially; a borderless imitation of native chrome buys little. Destroy the Dashboard/Builder webviews after closure, retaining compact navigation state in Rust. Test this lifecycle because operating-system webview resource release is not a contractual memory guarantee. [verified: https://v2.tauri.app/learn/system-tray/] **[assumption: lifecycle]**
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:46:Initial acceptance targets: after ten minutes with all windows closed, no Muse host/model/runtime worker remains; aggregate application-private footprint below 180 MiB on the reference Mac and 250 MiB on the reference Windows VM, with stable memory over eight hours. Report macOS physical footprint and Windows private working set separately, including observer and attributed webview processes; these metrics are not directly interchangeable. Target below 1% of one CPU core averaged over a static-screen interval; benchmark active work separately. Capture and OCR will likely dominate energy, so coalesce events, throttle frame processing, and suspend on lock/sleep. These are provisional budgets, not predictions. **[assumption]**
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:50:## 3. Component and process model
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:87:4. **Muse host.** One `muse serve` child per active build workspace and immutable sandbox posture, not a global unrestricted host. Reuse within that build only; stop after an idle timeout. MSP offers session operations and approvals, while serve's sandbox posture is fixed at host construction. Keep analysis-only jobs separate from shell-enabled build jobs. [local: plan/reference/muse-cli-help.txt; plan/reference/msp/msp.d.ts] **[assumption: host policy]**
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:94:## 4. IPC: private stdio JSON-RPC first
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:102:Own RPC contracts include `observer.setPolicy`, `observer.pause`, `analysis.submit`, `build.start`, `build.cancel`, `tool.invoke`, and `job.status`. Persist durable intent before acknowledging externally consequential actions. UI events carry sequence numbers and can be replaced by a snapshot after reconnect. Validate arguments on the Rust side even when TypeScript types exist. **[assumption]**
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:104:## 5. Muse, API keys, and the terminal
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:106:The repository records Muse Code 1.4.11, headless exec, stdio MSP, API-key precedence via META_API_KEY, and an observed structured rejection with a bogus key. It does **not** establish successful real-key use or serve environment authentication. Preserve that distinction. Do not invent an endpoint, npm SDK, model capability, or context limit. Discover model availability through the pinned host's catalog and expose a model picker; treat the locally observed model IDs as observations, not contractual availability. [local: plan/BRIEF.md; plan/reference/muse-cli-help.txt]
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:114:Use MSP for the main builder interface: progress, files changed, preview, test output, and approvals. The local schema distinguishes acknowledging presentation of an approval from actually deciding it, and exposes typed auth/quota errors. Follow those semantics; never turn a displayed approval into consent. [local: plan/reference/msp/msp.d.ts: RequestReceipt, SessionStartParams, QuotaDetail, TurnErrorKind]
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:118:## 6. Generated micro-apps and execution boundary
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:120:V1 supports static HTML/CSS/JS tools and restricted WASI command tools, not arbitrary always-on npm/Python servers. Serve each tool from its own loopback origin on an ephemeral port, with separate webview storage and no Tauri capability grant. Bind only loopback, reject invalid Host/Origin, disable remote navigation/popups/downloads, and deny external network by default through CSP plus broker policy. Do not grant generated windows wildcard product commands or inject product credentials. Tauri capabilities scope access by window/webview and have explicit limits; they do not sandbox arbitrary Rust or native children. [verified: https://v2.tauri.app/security/capabilities/] **[assumption: hosting design]**
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:126:Full native scripts are a later runtime tier requiring reviewed platform enforcement. Windows Job Objects manage process groups and limits; they are not a filesystem sandbox. AppContainer provides a separate isolation mechanism, whose compatibility must be proven. Do not label a child process plus a workspace path as a sandbox. [verified: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/procthread/job-objects] [verified: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/secauthz/appcontainer-isolation] **[assumption: scope boundary]**
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:128:## 7. Disk layout, configuration, and retention
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:155:## 8. Logs, recovery, and updates
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:157:Local structured logs contain timestamp, component/build version, job ID, duration, error code, and resource counters. Exclude screenshots, window titles, typed content, prompts, transcripts, terminal output, paths containing personal names, and secrets by default. Rotate to a 50 MiB total budget. Telemetry and crash upload are separate opt-ins, disabled initially. Diagnostic export shows an inspectable manifest; omit memory dumps because they can contain keys or frames. Display model usage/cost locally even when telemetry is off. **[assumption]**
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:167:## 9. Monorepo and AI-agent workflow
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:189:    tool-templates/              # HTML + WASI starter projects
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:190:  contracts/msp/                 # pinned vendor schemas + provenance/hash
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:202:Cargo workspace plus pnpm workspace; Swift Package Manager only for the Mac helper. Pin Rust, Node, package manager, Xcode expectations, MSP schema, and tested Muse versions. Provide one `doctor` command reporting missing SDKs, CLI compatibility, permission state, and credential presence without printing credentials. Give agents small contract-bound tasks, deterministic fixture inputs, and explicit ownership of native/security boundaries. Do not ask agents to rewrite transport, UI, and signing simultaneously. **[assumption]**
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:204:## 10. CI and build/release pipeline
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:208:Use Playwright browser tests for rapid UI behavior, plus packaged-app automation for IPC, real menus, focus, window creation, tool origin isolation, and PTY resizing. Browser WebKit tests do not establish WKWebView parity. Current Tauri documentation describes WebdriverIO's embedded server path on macOS/Windows; direct tauri-driver is limited to Windows/Linux. Keep embedded automation plugins debug/test-only and verify their absence from releases. Native capture permission tests still require an interactive signed session. [verified: https://v2.tauri.app/develop/tests/webdriver/] **[assumption: test strategy]**
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:210:Hosted CI cannot establish Windows capture, protected-app behavior, multi-monitor scaling, suspend, and permission UX. Add an interactive Windows VM/desktop test environment, screenshot/video artifacts, and recruited Windows beta testers. The owner can inspect recordings from the Mac. Windows release remains preview until that evidence exists; a green cross-platform build is insufficient. Test synthetic observation fixtures on every PR and signed real capture in scheduled platform sessions. **[assumption]**
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:215:2. Compile Rust executables, React assets, and Swift helper. Verify bundled sidecar versions/hashes. Initially detect a compatible user-installed Muse binary rather than redistribute it; installer and onboarding explain the dependency. Bundling requires redistribution rights and Windows availability verification.
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:217:4. On Windows, build on Windows, sign executables and installer through a managed signing identity, timestamp, verify signatures, and test clean install/uninstall/update including WebView2 provisioning. A valid signature is not a promise that reputation warnings never occur. [verified: https://v2.tauri.app/distribute/sign/windows/] **[assumption: validation policy]**
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:221:## 11. Product-killing risks and first proof
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:225:**Distribution:** ship direct-download notarized macOS builds, not a Mac App Store dependency. Apple's review guidelines restrict downloaded/executed code that changes functionality; the exact treatment of this product requires review, but a generated-tool architecture should not depend on a favorable store interpretation. Notarization is neither sandbox enforcement nor privacy certification. [verified: https://developer.apple.com/app-store/review/guidelines/] **[assumption: distribution choice]**
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:227:**Meta dependency/cost:** successful API-key auth for serve, provider terms for captured third-party data, Muse redistribution, and Windows binary/sandbox support remain explicit gates. Do not route screen data to an unverified endpoint or build a business model on unverified prices/context sizes. Enforce queue concurrency and admission budgets locally, while admitting that a running provider turn may overshoot a locally estimated budget. **[assumption]** [local: plan/BRIEF.md]
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:229:First proof: a signed Tauri app with real tray Pause/Quit, a separate Swift helper emitting sanitized fixtures and one consented capture, an eight-hour idle measurement, two real windows, one isolated HTML tool, and a real-key Muse run. In parallel CI terms, build the same shell and synthetic observer contract on Windows; then obtain an interactive Windows test session. If these fail, resolve the specific boundary before building the pattern engine. **[assumption]**
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:231:## Open questions for the owner
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:233:- Can v1 require Apple Silicon/macOS 14+ and Windows 11 x64, with Windows explicitly preview until interactive beta coverage exists?
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:235:- Is HTML plus restricted WASI sufficient for first pinned tools, with unrestricted native Python/shell deployment deferred?
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:236:- What monthly API spending ceiling should onboarding default to, and can sanitized evidence leave the device only after per-workflow review initially?
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:238:## Decisions I made
