plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:9:Choose **(a) Tauri 2**, with **React + TypeScript + Vite**, a Rust application core, and a signed Swift observer helper on macOS. Implement the Windows observer using Rust and Windows APIs. Do not build two native application interfaces, embed Flutter, or ship a permanent Python/Node service. This recommendation prioritizes low background overhead and one maintainable interface while accepting WebKit/WebView2 differences and some native integration work. **[assumption]**
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:11:Tauri's core manages windows and tray interfaces; its web content uses operating-system webviews, specifically WKWebView on macOS and WebView2 on Windows. It does not bundle those webview libraries. That establishes the structural opportunity to keep a small native resident core and destroy unused interface windows; it does **not** prove a particular RAM saving. [verified: https://v2.tauri.app/concept/process-model/] [verified: https://v2.tauri.app/reference/webview-versions/]
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:13:React wins over Svelte/Solid here because I expect the broadest useful agent-generated examples, UI components, terminal integrations, and test fixtures. That is a productivity judgment, not a measured model benchmark. Keep React out of the capture and execution layers. Prefer ordinary hooks and a small state store to a large application framework; no SSR server. Pin dependencies and build reproducibly from lockfiles. **[assumption]**
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:17:The following assessments are architectural judgments **[assumption]**, grounded where noted in documented platform capabilities.
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:23:| Embedded terminal | xterm.js directly in existing UI | Best fit for xterm.js and familiar editor patterns | Dart terminal or another webview bridge | Native terminal library or webview on each platform |
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:24:| Micro-app hosting | Existing web stack, separate unprivileged host windows | Consistent Chromium; strong embedding primitives | Desktop webview coverage becomes another dependency | Separate WKWebView/WebView2 integration |
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:26:| Idle footprint | Favorable architecture if windows/workers truly exit | Chromium/Node baseline is an accepted cost | Resident rendering engine plus webviews for web tools | Best potential minimum; more UI code |
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:28:| Windows parity | Same UI, different browser/native capture implementation | Strongest rendering consistency | Same Flutter UI; web embedding/native gaps remain | Lowest probability of maintained parity for one Mac owner |
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:30:| Testing | Strong core tests; real webview and native tests necessary | Consistent browser automation; native helpers still separate | Strong widget tests; bridge/platform integration tests | Strong native tests, doubled interface coverage |
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:32:Electron is the close second, and its advantage is substantial: its documented main/renderer model supplies a known Chromium-based environment, with a renderer for each BrowserWindow. For a terminal-heavy IDE, I would accept its baseline cost. Macroscope spends most hours observing with no visible editor. Choosing Electron to improve the minority editing workload would make its permanent runtime a product trade-off; I choose Tauri instead. No claim that every Tauri app uses less memory is warranted. [verified: https://www.electronjs.org/docs/latest/tutorial/process-model] **[assumption: product weighting]**
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:34:For generated content, Electron recommends alternatives such as WebContentsView rather than its webview tag, and its security guide cautions about untrusted content and privileged APIs. Electron would still need a deliberately constrained bridge and execution policy; switching frameworks does not solve generated-code security. [verified: https://www.electronjs.org/docs/latest/api/webview-tag] [verified: https://www.electronjs.org/docs/latest/tutorial/security]
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:36:Flutter is credible because the owner knows it and it supports Windows/macOS desktop. flutter_rust_bridge provides the Dart/Rust binding route. However, the official webview_flutter package currently lists Android, iOS, and macOS, leaving Windows web hosting to a different integration. A Flutter shell plus Rust plus native capture plus web-hosted tools creates more boundaries than this product needs. Reject it for this app, not for desktop generally. [verified: https://docs.flutter.dev/platform-integration/desktop] [verified: https://github.com/fzyzcjy/flutter_rust_bridge] [verified: https://pub.dev/packages/webview_flutter] **[assumption: complexity assessment]**
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:38:SwiftUI/WinUI gives the best native polish and smallest plausible idle UI, but makes the owner responsible for two interface implementations, two accessibility behaviors, two terminal hosts, and two sets of UI tests. Shared Rust cannot share those obligations. Reject that maintenance load. Also reject a Python/Qt alternative: Python remains useful for experiments, but adds packaging/runtime work without an advantage over the selected stack for web tools. **[assumption]**
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:42:The native tray menu contains: Observing/Paused, Pause for 15 minutes, Open Macroscope, Pending approvals, and Quit Macroscope. A recording state must come from the observer's acknowledged state, never an optimistic toggle. Closing the dashboard leaves observation running; Quit stops capture, workers, terminals, and schedules. Login launch is explicit opt-in and starts only the native shell plus observer. No hidden machine-wide service, root daemon, or Windows administrator requirement. **[assumption]**
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:44:Tauri documents native tray construction and menu callbacks in Rust; implement those there so no webview is necessary for Pause or Quit. Create Dashboard, Builder, and each pinned tool as distinct logical windows. Keep ordinary title bars and standard keyboard shortcuts initially; a borderless imitation of native chrome buys little. Destroy the Dashboard/Builder webviews after closure, retaining compact navigation state in Rust. Test this lifecycle because operating-system webview resource release is not a contractual memory guarantee. [verified: https://v2.tauri.app/learn/system-tray/] **[assumption: lifecycle]**
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:46:Initial acceptance targets: after ten minutes with all windows closed, no Muse host/model/runtime worker remains; aggregate application-private footprint below 180 MiB on the reference Mac and 250 MiB on the reference Windows VM, with stable memory over eight hours. Report macOS physical footprint and Windows private working set separately, including observer and attributed webview processes; these metrics are not directly interchangeable. Target below 1% of one CPU core averaged over a static-screen interval; benchmark active work separately. Capture and OCR will likely dominate energy, so coalesce events, throttle frame processing, and suspend on lock/sleep. These are provisional budgets, not predictions. **[assumption]**
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:84:1. **Resident core / UI process boundary.** The Tauri Rust core is the per-user coordinator and sole database writer. OS webview subprocesses render UI. The core contains no OCR loop, model loop, or generated code. It owns the real tray menu, user intent, capability grants, and worker startup. Keeping this coordinator resident avoids inventing a second always-on supervisor. **[assumption]**
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:100:Frames remain in the observer until sanitized. Small sanitized JPEGs can initially cross as bounded base64 payloads; large media should use authenticated, length-delimited binary pipes with metadata references once measurements justify it. Do not stream raw pixels through JSON or send machine-wide paths to frontend callers. Drop/coalesce capture backlog; never block capture on a webview. Critical Pause/Quit control gets a separate priority queue. **[assumption]**
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:104:## 5. Muse, API keys, and the terminal
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:106:The repository records Muse Code 1.4.11, headless exec, stdio MSP, API-key precedence via META_API_KEY, and an observed structured rejection with a bogus key. It does **not** establish successful real-key use or serve environment authentication. Preserve that distinction. Do not invent an endpoint, npm SDK, model capability, or context limit. Discover model availability through the pinned host's catalog and expose a model picker; treat the locally observed model IDs as observations, not contractual availability. [local: plan/BRIEF.md; plan/reference/muse-cli-help.txt]
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:110:For direct Meta requests, the core retrieves the key and sends it through a dedicated inherited credential pipe to the model worker; the adapter supplies the provider's documented authorization scheme after verification. For `muse exec --api-key-stdin`, a separate one-shot child receives it through stdin. For `muse serve --provider meta`, launch with an explicitly constructed environment containing META_API_KEY; its stdin must remain exclusively MSP. Never use argv, a checked-in dotenv file, terminal export commands, or consumer login. On key rotation kill the old host and start a new one. [local: plan/reference/muse-cli-help.txt; plan/BRIEF.md] **[assumption: serve path pending verification]**
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:116:Use **xterm.js** only for an advanced terminal drawer backed by a real PTY/ConPTY adapter in Rust. Native terminal widgets would require two integration paths; an MSP userShell request is a command operation, not proof of an interactive PTY. Initially the terminal is a user-started workspace shell; Muse runs headlessly through MSP. Disable automatic clipboard/escape integrations and URL opening, bound scrollback, and keep terminal bytes off analytics. xterm's security guidance explicitly treats terminal access and privileges as application responsibilities. [verified: https://xtermjs.org/docs/guides/security/] [local: plan/reference/msp/msp.d.ts: SessionUserShellParams] **[assumption: terminal design]**
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:120:V1 supports static HTML/CSS/JS tools and restricted WASI command tools, not arbitrary always-on npm/Python servers. Serve each tool from its own loopback origin on an ephemeral port, with separate webview storage and no Tauri capability grant. Bind only loopback, reject invalid Host/Origin, disable remote navigation/popups/downloads, and deny external network by default through CSP plus broker policy. Do not grant generated windows wildcard product commands or inject product credentials. Tauri capabilities scope access by window/webview and have explicit limits; they do not sandbox arbitrary Rust or native children. [verified: https://v2.tauri.app/security/capabilities/] **[assumption: hosting design]**
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:122:A custom bridge issues short-lived, tool-specific invocation tokens for named actions, never general shell commands. Broker HTTP requests to approved domains and expose user-selected file handles/working copies. A web app's fetch restrictions alone do not constrain an associated native executable. Distinct webviews/origins are logical security boundaries; do not assume a separate OS process for every webview. **[assumption]**
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:124:Use Wasmtime inside the executor for bounded command tools: explicit directory preopens, no ambient credentials/network, memory and execution limits, and output staging before user-approved export. Wasmtime documents WASI filesystem capability isolation. This does not make every host callback safe; test path traversal, symlinks, resource exhaustion, and broker authorization. [verified: https://docs.wasmtime.dev/security.html] **[assumption: runtime policy]**
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:157:Local structured logs contain timestamp, component/build version, job ID, duration, error code, and resource counters. Exclude screenshots, window titles, typed content, prompts, transcripts, terminal output, paths containing personal names, and secrets by default. Rotate to a 50 MiB total budget. Telemetry and crash upload are separate opt-ins, disabled initially. Diagnostic export shows an inspectable manifest; omit memory dumps because they can contain keys or frames. Display model usage/cost locally even when telemetry is off. **[assumption]**
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:159:Job lifecycle: queued, admitted, running, awaitingApproval, completed, failed, interrupted, outcomeUnknown. Persist run ID, input/artifact hashes, capability revision, and external-action receipts. On restart, mark unfinished externally consequential runs outcomeUnknown; never automatically replay a send/payment/file overwrite. Resume Muse sessions only after reconciling the durable session snapshot and pending approvals; do not infer success from the last streamed delta. **[assumption]**
