1:# Macroscope master build plan
5:This document supersedes competing recommendations in the research reports. Read in full: [BRIEF](BRIEF.md) and all fifteen files in [research](research/): 01 product, 02 competition, 03 Meta models, 04 Muse/MSP, 05 alternative builders, 06 macOS capture, 07 Windows capture, 08 privacy/security, 09 patterns, 10 runtime, 11 and 12 architecture, 13 and 14 UX, and 15 cost/evaluations. Their conflicting proposals are resolved below rather than accumulated into one oversized MVP.
14:## 0. Summary and key decisions
16:Build a personal desktop utility that turns a deliberately shown or described task into a small, tested tool. Its first useful output is a CSV/text/file transformation that runs deterministically without a model. The first session must deliver value before requesting broad observation. Subsequently, opt-in local discovery identifies recurring work and asks the user to teach or confirm the actual rule. The durable product object is a versioned tool with a behavior contract, proof results, permissions, and run receipts.
20:Implement Tauri 2/React/TypeScript, Rust coordination and policy, a Swift macOS observer, and a native Rust Windows observer. Integrate Muse through MSP over child stdio. Generate TypeScript against a small effect-broker SDK and run it in pinned Deno with direct effects denied. Keep a transcript and advanced terminal available in Builder, while making the corrected task contract and test output the primary review surfaces.
26:### Key decisions
30:| Topic | Chosen | Rejected option | Why |
32:| Initial wedge | Show/Tell and deterministic output | Ambient discovery as first-run dependency (02/13) | A useful first-session tool tests value without weeks of capture. |
33:| Discovery scope | Later opt-in local metadata mining with conservative cards | No discovery indefinitely (01), continuous cloud interpretation (03/15) | Discovery preserves the original thesis while local mining bounds exposure and spend. |
34:| Product structure | Discoveries, Tools, Builds, Activity, Privacy; compact menu access | Drawer-only Run/Teach/Review model (14), chat-first app | Persistent destinations make costs, permissions, repair, and evidence inspectable as the library grows. |
35:| Task understanding | Editable structured Task Contract | Transcript as authoritative specification | A corrected contract makes business rules and acceptance criteria explicit before generation. |
36:| Desktop stack | Tauri 2 + React/TS + Rust | Electron, Flutter, SwiftUI plus WinUI | One web interface and a native resident core fit a solo developer's background utility. |
37:| Mac observer | Swift process in signed bundle | Rust-first Apple framework bindings (12) | Direct Apple APIs reduce capture, TCC, and lifecycle integration work. |
38:| IPC | Inherited pipes; XPC for Mac observer identity | General UDS/gRPC services (12) | The parent-child topology needs no discoverable network service. |
39:| Database ownership | Core is sole persistent writer | Observer/engine/shell each own separate writable DB (12) | Central migrations and deletion lineage are simpler at the selected ingest volume. |
40:| Generated artifact | TypeScript SDK workflow in Deno | Custom graph DSL (05), WASI-first tools (11), Python-first scripts (01) | TypeScript preserves expressive transformations while the broker owns effects. |
41:| Runtime authority | All user-data reads and effects through broker | Direct filesystem reads for generated Deno (10) | Brokered reads prevent code from bypassing previews, limits, and audit policy. |
42:| Builder integration | MSP primary; bounded exec adapter for recovery | PTY scraping, a new general coding loop | MSP exposes actual sessions, items, approval stages, and typed failures. |
43:| Muse hosting | One active restricted host per posture; one active build globally | Per-tool permanent hosts, network-enabled default host | Reuse limits startup cost while serial builds simplify accounting and workspace isolation. |
44:| Build shell | Disabled in MVP; host-owned tests | Key-bearing unrestricted agent shell (04/12) | The observed inherited key makes arbitrary shell execution an avoidable credential risk. |
45:| Terminal | Key-free workspace shell; Muse TUI gated later | Automatically keyed login shell, always-on dual writers | Debugging should not expose the billing key or create competing session owners. |
46:| Sandbox assertions | Demonstrated enforcement or block capability | Workspace isolation, Job Objects, or static scans as sandbox proof | None independently constrains every filesystem or network effect. |
47:| Consent | Capture, egress, build tools, and runtime grants separate | One onboarding approval for everything | Recording an action does not authorize a model request or its future execution. |
48:| Capture defaults | Manual five-minute session; native selected window; browsers excluded initially | Always-on screenshots, title heuristics for private mode | Narrow scope is testable and fails closed when private context cannot be established. |
49:| Keyboard/clipboard | No keyboard monitoring; clipboard only explicit run input | Rhythm hooks, clipboard HMAC observer (06/09) | The first supported workflows do not justify keylogger-shaped collection. |
50:| Image/model policy | No image upload by default; reviewed crops to analyzer only | Cloud per-frame vision, raw screenshots to coding agent | Structure and synthetic fixtures suffice for the first buildable workflows. |
51:| Models | Spark for bounded interpretation; Wonder for requested builds/repairs | Weekly Wonder mining, local Glimmer, Contributor discounts | Rare high-value generation warrants Wonder while API-only Standard use meets the brief. |
52:| Costs | Sparse discovery; about $11/month baseline build workload | $19 text-every-episode, $43 cloud-heavy, $32 continuous model plan | Most episodes need no paid interpretation before a local candidate exists. |
53:| Retention | Images 24 h; sanitized events 7 d; aggregates 30 d | Seven-day images, 90-day detailed events, indefinite episodes | Short evidence retention is adequate for teaching and limits the sensitive archive. |
54:| Repair/trust | Explicit repair, re-test, re-approve; promotion offered after three clean runs | Silent selector repair/auto-promotion (10), five-run rule (13) | A repaired program changes behavior and must earn renewed authority. |
55:| MVP integrations | Files/text/tables; manual/hotkey; drafts as files | Browser acting, arbitrary AppleScript/PowerShell, runtime LLMs | Reversible local outputs give the strongest early correctness oracle. |
56:| Distribution | Developer ID/notarized direct Mac; signed MSIX Windows | Mac App Store, Windows NSIS-first, bundled Muse | Native observation needs direct distribution and MSIX supports the chosen Windows OCR route. |
57:| Platforms | Apple Silicon macOS 15/26; Windows 11 x64 later | macOS 14/Intel/universal and Windows ARM parity immediately | Supported targets require hardware evidence the owner currently lacks. |
58:| Billing | Each external tester supplies their own key | Shipping the owner's key, bundled credits in v1 | BYOK avoids a proxy/data-processing service and shared-key abuse. |
