plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:87:4. **Muse host.** One `muse serve` child per active build workspace and immutable sandbox posture, not a global unrestricted host. Reuse within that build only; stop after an idle timeout. MSP offers session operations and approvals, while serve's sandbox posture is fixed at host construction. Keep analysis-only jobs separate from shell-enabled build jobs. [local: plan/reference/muse-cli-help.txt; plan/reference/msp/msp.d.ts] **[assumption: host policy]**
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:116:Use **xterm.js** only for an advanced terminal drawer backed by a real PTY/ConPTY adapter in Rust. Native terminal widgets would require two integration paths; an MSP userShell request is a command operation, not proof of an interactive PTY. Initially the terminal is a user-started workspace shell; Muse runs headlessly through MSP. Disable automatic clipboard/escape integrations and URL opening, bound scrollback, and keep terminal bytes off analytics. xterm's security guidance explicitly treats terminal access and privileges as application responsibilities. [verified: https://xtermjs.org/docs/guides/security/] [local: plan/reference/msp/msp.d.ts: SessionUserShellParams] **[assumption: terminal design]**
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:120:V1 supports static HTML/CSS/JS tools and restricted WASI command tools, not arbitrary always-on npm/Python servers. Serve each tool from its own loopback origin on an ephemeral port, with separate webview storage and no Tauri capability grant. Bind only loopback, reject invalid Host/Origin, disable remote navigation/popups/downloads, and deny external network by default through CSP plus broker policy. Do not grant generated windows wildcard product commands or inject product credentials. Tauri capabilities scope access by window/webview and have explicit limits; they do not sandbox arbitrary Rust or native children. [verified: https://v2.tauri.app/security/capabilities/] **[assumption: hosting design]**
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:122:A custom bridge issues short-lived, tool-specific invocation tokens for named actions, never general shell commands. Broker HTTP requests to approved domains and expose user-selected file handles/working copies. A web app's fetch restrictions alone do not constrain an associated native executable. Distinct webviews/origins are logical security boundaries; do not assume a separate OS process for every webview. **[assumption]**
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:126:Full native scripts are a later runtime tier requiring reviewed platform enforcement. Windows Job Objects manage process groups and limits; they are not a filesystem sandbox. AppContainer provides a separate isolation mechanism, whose compatibility must be proven. Do not label a child process plus a workspace path as a sandbox. [verified: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/procthread/job-objects] [verified: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/secauthz/appcontainer-isolation] **[assumption: scope boundary]**
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:208:Use Playwright browser tests for rapid UI behavior, plus packaged-app automation for IPC, real menus, focus, window creation, tool origin isolation, and PTY resizing. Browser WebKit tests do not establish WKWebView parity. Current Tauri documentation describes WebdriverIO's embedded server path on macOS/Windows; direct tauri-driver is limited to Windows/Linux. Keep embedded automation plugins debug/test-only and verify their absence from releases. Native capture permission tests still require an interactive signed session. [verified: https://v2.tauri.app/develop/tests/webdriver/] **[assumption: test strategy]**
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:225:**Distribution:** ship direct-download notarized macOS builds, not a Mac App Store dependency. Apple's review guidelines restrict downloaded/executed code that changes functionality; the exact treatment of this product requires review, but a generated-tool architecture should not depend on a favorable store interpretation. Notarization is neither sandbox enforcement nor privacy certification. [verified: https://developer.apple.com/app-store/review/guidelines/] **[assumption: distribution choice]**
plan/research/11-architecture-codex.md:227:**Meta dependency/cost:** successful API-key auth for serve, provider terms for captured third-party data, Muse redistribution, and Windows binary/sandbox support remain explicit gates. Do not route screen data to an unverified endpoint or build a business model on unverified prices/context sizes. Enforce queue concurrency and admission budgets locally, while admitting that a running provider turn may overshoot a locally estimated budget. **[assumption]** [local: plan/BRIEF.md]
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:9:Ship **text-first**: local capture, OCR, Accessibility (AX) extraction, redaction, deduplication, and episode segmentation; Muse Spark for episode interpretation and opportunity ranking; Muse Wonder for explicitly requested builds. Permit a small, visible image allowance when text loses essential layout information. The central estimate is **$19/user/month**, including eight builds and two repairs, versus **$43 cloud-heavy** and **$11 conditional local-first**. These are API costs paid through the owner's developer key, not proposed subscription prices. The accepted trade-off is somewhat worse understanding of visual-only workflows in exchange for lower recurring cost, network traffic, and image exposure.
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:42:Token-equivalents assume four bytes of mostly English text per token solely for sizing. They are not measured Muse token counts. Events contain action categories, timing, app identity and safe control labels, not raw keystrokes or unrestricted clipboard contents. OCR/AX still contain sensitive text and must be redacted. Frame candidates are transient buffers, not 21,600 encoded screenshots written to disk. Animation, scrolling and video must not defeat the processing ceiling; excessive change triggers sampling rather than a backlog.
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:69:Build and repair workloads are common to all three designs:
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:77:These totals cover complete bounded runs, including their internal revisions. A later repair is a distinct run. A build request averages $1.22 including specification. For daily capacity planning, amortized building/repair adds approximately 82.2k input and 8.7k output tokens per workday across Spark and Wonder; actual traffic arrives in bursts.
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:82:C_month = 22 * C_observation_day + sum(builds) + sum(repairs) + tool_fees
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:92:| Builds + repairs, common | $1.86 | $10.69 | $70.26 |
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:97:Light/busy observation is 0.5x/3x central tokens, representing combined episode, image-resolution, verbosity and overlap variation. It is a sensitivity envelope, not a measured percentile. Light building is four $0.40 coding runs, four $0.016 specifications, and one $0.20 repair. Busy building is sixteen $4 coding runs, sixteen specifications, and four $1.50 repairs. Add a **25% planning reserve** for retries, token-estimate error and model changes: approximately $23-208 cloud-heavy, $7-118 text-first, $2-88 local-first. This reserve is not proof against runaway loops.
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:101:Local-first's zero observation API cost excludes hardware, electricity, model downloads and slower discovery. Other excluded costs include signing/distribution, developer CI and local test execution. Keep v1 recordings and sandbox execution on-device, with no hosted browser fleet. If paid execution infrastructure is introduced, budget it separately per sandbox minute; these API totals are not total service cost.
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:166:                                       frozen build contract + sandbox
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:168:                                       hidden tests --> fault --> repair tests
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:193:For each task, freeze an acceptance contract and independent tests before generation. Include exact output files, malformed-input behavior, permitted files/network destinations, idempotency and rollback. Keep some tests hidden from the agent; generated tests are useful diagnostics but cannot be their own success oracle. Run in a clean sandbox, with fake services, temporary workspaces and disposable accounts. Pass only when all required tests pass within the same cost/time limits used in the app, with no forbidden writes/network effects.
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:203:Take known-good tools and inject one fault at a time: changed DOM label, reordered column, missing optional value, API 429, expired credential, changed response schema and dependency mismatch. Preserve the original acceptance tests; add a fault-specific oracle outside the builder's workspace. Include valid tools as negative controls so an unnecessary repair can be counted. Authentication failures should lead to an actionable reconnect state, not generated credential workarounds.
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:205:Count repair success only if the original behavior and new fault test both pass without wider permissions, destructive effects or unauthorized dependency changes. Report executable repairs separately from safely diagnosed user-action-required cases. Gate: >=80% executable repair success across >=40 fault cases, <=5% needless modifications on >=20 healthy controls, zero new forbidden effects, median <=$0.50 and p90 <=$1.50/repair, at most two attempts. Unsupported changes must stop clearly rather than loop.
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:217:Run cheap redaction, serialization, resource and sandbox regressions on every relevant PR. Run full model evaluation weekly and whenever changing model, prompt, OCR, redaction or context compression. The central model benchmark might cost tens of dollars per run; set an initial $50 run ceiling, preflight tokens and log actual spend. Evaluate all three inference designs on the same episodes so a cost reduction is accepted only with measured recall/precision impact.
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:221:Every numerical gate above is a proposed acceptance threshold, not an achieved score. Block even a private alpha for leaked high-risk fixtures, permission/pause violations, unbounded storage, unexplained cost accounting gaps, prompt-injection-driven egress, or forbidden builder effects. Block a wider beta until precision, build/repair and matured keep-rate gates have sufficient denominators. Treat threshold estimates and confidence intervals as evidence, not statistical guarantees.
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:225:Validate capture and sandboxed building in the signed distribution build before release: App Store sandbox constraints, notarization/distribution choices and OS permission behavior could invalidate development-build measurements. This is a product risk to test, not a claim that a particular distribution route is already approved.
