plan/research/09-pattern-engine.md:126:- Read field role and label, never value. Labels can themselves contain personal data: redact them and default unknown labels to `[FIELD]`. Secure fields and excluded contexts emit no text, clipboard, AX, frame, or OCR details.
plan/research/09-pattern-engine.md:127:- Record shortcuts from a fixed command vocabulary, no ordinary keys. Normalize Cmd/Ctrl to `primary`, preserving platform separately. A copy shortcut is an attempted command, not proof of clipboard change or successful paste.
plan/research/09-pattern-engine.md:129:- Clipboard observation is opt-in. Canonicalize approved text representations in memory, compute a device-secret HMAC, then discard bytes. Daily key epochs limit linkage; correlate primarily by clipboard generation. Plain hashes make small amounts guessable. Do not export hashes, clipboard IDs, or exact lengths to models. Previews default off, limited to 80 sanitized characters when enabled; images and files receive type metadata only.
plan/research/09-pattern-engine.md:137:[assumption] Hard-close episodes on lock, pause, sleep, permission loss, or 120 seconds idle. An app/window/tab switch starts a provisional segment. Continue the episode when the previous context returns within 45 seconds, a clipboard edge crosses the switch, or a known document/file relation connects it. Otherwise close at the switch after 45 seconds of unrelated activity. Use a 30-minute episode cap with continuation links. These boundaries have explicit reasons and confidence; they are revisable. Ambiguous segments remain separate, with candidate mining permitted across adjacent segments only when a dataflow or high-confidence task link exists. Never bridge privacy gaps.
plan/research/09-pattern-engine.md:150:[assumption] Collapse focus-click-focus into one activation; combine shortcut plus clipboard notification into one copy; combine paste plus field focus into one paste. Preserve undo/retry as annotations rather than silently removing them. Clipboard hash/generation match plus timing and focused target supports a transfer edge; mere Cmd+V supports `paste_attempt`. Retain observed and inferred actions distinctly. Never infer cell B2 from screen coordinates alone.
plan/research/09-pattern-engine.md:178:4. **Handle gaps:** add constrained PrefixSpan for longer sequences after exact mining works. PrefixSpan grows sequential patterns using projected prefixes [verified: original paper, https://hanj.cs.illinois.edu/pdf/span01.pdf]. Our variant allows at most three skipped actions, 30 seconds between matched actions, ten minutes total span, and no protected boundary crossing. Retain matched intervals, not just subsequences; demand >=70% action coverage to avoid matching arbitrary browser activity. Stop at 10,000 candidates or 60 seconds per job and resume later.
plan/research/09-pattern-engine.md:181:[assumption] Weekly periodicity uses local weekday/time-of-day histograms with 30-minute bins and +/-60-minute tolerance. Label “usually Tuesdays around 2pm” only after four observed weeks, >=75% matching weeks, and concentration above the user's overall activity baseline. Track recording coverage: an unobserved Tuesday is unknown, not a missed workflow. Preserve original timezone and handle DST in local time. Multiple jobs in a Tuesday batch count as one schedule occurrence. A schedule raises explanatory relevance, never establishes repetition or automatically creates a trigger.
plan/research/09-pattern-engine.md:229:[assumption] The model returns only cluster name, tentative goal, observed-versus-inferred explanation, unresolved questions, and an approach referencing existing capability IDs. Reject nonexistent evidence, capability IDs, or unsupported factual claims. One invalid response gets one repair within the daily allowance; then use a deterministic label. The model cannot increase measured Q, support, savings, or A. Authentication/quota errors become actionable UI states; no consumer login fallback and no automatic replay storm.
plan/research/09-pattern-engine.md:240:          "origin":"proposed","user_confirmed":false},
plan/research/09-pattern-engine.md:354:[assumption] Build a consented, local corpus by recording the developer doing 12 workflow families with eight executions each across six weeks: spreadsheet-to-form transfer, download/rename/import, weekly report, expense draft, file conversion, and native-app equivalents. Include exceptions, interruptions, IME typing, permission denial, and unrelated browsing. Add at least 100 negative episodes: research, creative writing, navigation, and coincidental copy/paste. Use sandbox accounts and fabricated business data. Hand-label episode boundaries, task identity, essential steps, transfers, correct automation scope, and reasonable savings. A second reviewer checks 20%; owner-only labels are useful development evidence, not a product-quality estimate.
plan/research/09-pattern-engine.md:356:[assumption] Generate >=1,000 reproducible synthetic trace variants from labeled traces: insert distractions, drop events, duplicate notifications, change labels/rows, invert transfers, reorder timestamps, simulate clipboard overwrite, midnight/DST, batch loops, and download renames. Plant secrets and malicious OCR instructions to test leakage and injection. Synthetic quality measures robustness, not market demand. Split by whole workflow family and time; never put perturbed copies of the same seed in train and test.
plan/research/09-pattern-engine.md:366:[assumption] Log feedback against opportunity revision and evidence. “Not repetitive” corrects cluster membership and suppresses the same pattern until materially new evidence; “Wrong steps” requests local boundary/mapping correction; “Not worth it” lowers that user's threshold preference; “Later” snoozes 30 days without a negative label. Accept is a positive relevance signal, not proof of feasibility. Failed builds lower the affected capability prior; successful runs update measured savings after subtracting review/repair time. Keep the original baseline and note workload changes to avoid attributing unrelated speedups to automation.
plan/research/09-pattern-engine.md:372:[assumption] The largest risk is insufficient identity: pixels and click coordinates may never reveal stable source/destination semantics. Ship a narrow adapter-supported workflow set first and abstain elsewhere. Permission loss or weak Windows coverage must be visible, not compensated for by model confidence. Privacy failures in labels, OCR, and clipboard can destroy trust; redaction and egress tests precede cloud inference. Screen prompt injection must have no route to tools or authority. Meta model availability, API-key success, provider retention/terms, quotas, and actual pricing remain launch gates; the local engine must work when inference is unavailable. Capture/helper distribution, notarization, and possible store restrictions require a separate platform review; no store eligibility is assumed here.
plan/research/09-pattern-engine.md:379:- Is opt-in clipboard metadata/HMAC correlation acceptable, or should v1 rely on shortcut and field evidence until a teach session?
plan/research/06-capture-macos.md:9:**[assumption]** Build a small, unsandboxed Swift observer, shipped inside a Developer ID-signed, notarized desktop app. Support macOS 15 and 26 explicitly. Prefer structured browser events, then Accessibility structure, then occasional redacted screenshots. Observe only the foreground allowed application/window. Do not record audio, clipboard contents, typed characters, background tabs, or all displays by default. This trades comprehensive replay for a defensible, inexpensive workflow signal.
plan/research/06-capture-macos.md:14:CGEventTap ----- click + aggregate typing/shortcut hints -+--> Swift observer
plan/research/06-capture-macos.md:76:**[assumption]** Do not read editable `AXValue`, selected text, document body, or clipboard payloads. Window titles, document URLs, labels, and AX descriptions are also potentially sensitive; sanitize before persistence and use title fingerprints where literal text adds no workflow value. Retain structural hashes and capped deltas rather than repeated whole trees. For click targeting, perform an asynchronous `AXUIElementCopyElementAtPosition` and attach it only if the foreground generation still agrees.
plan/research/06-capture-macos.md:88:**[assumption]** Count ordinary keys into one-second buckets, then retain burst duration and coarse cadence rather than individual timings. Recognize only an explicit shortcut allowlist, such as Copy, Paste, Save, Undo, Find, and Tab/Return navigation; discard unrecognized chords. Do not classify every Option combination as a shortcut because some produce text. Record Copy/Paste actions without clipboard content. Quantize click coordinates relative to the active window and prefer a sanitized control identity. Handle tap-disabled notifications with bounded re-enable attempts and a visible health state. Denial disables this source; it does not justify switching APIs to evade permission.
plan/research/06-capture-macos.md:108:**[assumption]** Yes: an extension is a better web-event source than screenshots. Start with Chromium MV3 and reuse its TypeScript event schema in Safari Web Extensions. Ask for optional access to selected work origins, not browsing history or every site. `activeTab` supports a manual demonstration; ongoing observation requires durable permissions for the approved origins. Native observation remains useful for desktop tools and cross-app transitions.
plan/research/06-capture-macos.md:112:**[assumption]** Sanitize URLs in the extension and again natively: remove userinfo, query and fragment by default; tokenize variable path segments with a keyed local hash. A path can contain a customer name or secret too. Send only origin/template and pseudonymous entity equality unless an app adapter explicitly permits more. Observe cross-origin frames only with their own granted origin; mark closed shadow DOM, canvas, internal browser pages, PDFs, and denied frames as coverage gaps. No debugger permission or remote-debugging port for v1.
plan/research/06-capture-macos.md:114:Native messaging uses a registered host, length-prefixed JSON over stdio, and fixed `allowed_origins`; Chrome documents a user-specific macOS manifest location. Content scripts route messages through the extension service worker. Safari provides an app-extension/native messaging bridge. **[verified: https://developer.chrome.com/docs/extensions/develop/concepts/native-messaging]** **[verified: https://developer.apple.com/documentation/safariservices/messaging-a-web-extension-s-native-app]**
plan/research/06-capture-macos.md:116:Apple explicitly supports Developer ID-signed, notarized Safari Web Extension distribution outside the Mac App Store. The companion therefore need not force the unsandboxed observer into App Store distribution. **[verified: https://developer.apple.com/documentation/safariservices/distributing-your-safari-web-extension]**
plan/research/06-capture-macos.md:118:**[assumption]** Use a tiny signed native messaging adapter connected to the same observer, not a local unauthenticated HTTP server. Install the manifest only when enabling browser integration; support uninstall. Validate extension identity, message size, sender origin, schema, document generation, and policy version. Browser-controlled tab metadata is authoritative for URLs/private flags; page-supplied fields are untrusted. Validate labels and cap them. Add sequence acknowledgments and bounded reconnect queues; no unbounded content buffering while the desktop app is offline.
plan/research/06-capture-macos.md:180:Mac App Store guideline 2.4.5 requires sandboxing. Apple lists assistive use of Accessibility APIs and sending Apple Events to arbitrary apps among sandbox-incompatible activities. **Conclusion: this broad observer is unsuitable for the Mac App Store; screen recording alone is not categorically forbidden there.** **[verified: https://developer.apple.com/app-store/review/guidelines/]** **[verified: https://developer.apple.com/documentation/security/protecting-user-data-with-app-sandbox]**
plan/research/06-capture-macos.md:188:**[assumption]** Put Screen Recording purpose text on the actual capture identity, and `NSAppleEventsUsageDescription` plus the Automation entitlement only on the executable implementing the optional URL adapter. Keep the observer free of JIT, unsigned executable memory, library-validation exceptions, microphone, camera, and persistent-capture entitlements. Screen capture and AX still require user consent; do not invent a `com.apple.security.screen-capture` entitlement. Audit UI-framework-specific hardened-runtime exceptions separately so a UI runtime does not widen the helper's permissions.
plan/research/06-capture-macos.md:225:- No typed text, ordinary keycodes, clipboard payloads, audio, or raw persistent frame buffer.
plan/research/07-capture-windows.md:13:**[assumption]** Use an MSIX-packaged full-trust desktop app distributed directly during beta. Package identity is useful for supported OCR and borderless capture capabilities. “Full trust” here is a packaging term, not a request for administrator rights. Run the UI, native observer, and model/generation host as separate processes with separate responsibilities:
plan/research/07-capture-windows.md:82:**[assumption]** Retain roles, relationships, bounds, focus/enabled/selected/password flags, supported pattern names, and sanitized labels. Do not dump `ValuePattern.Value`, document `TextPattern` ranges, clipboard contents, or edit control text. UIA `Name`, AutomationId, window titles, and labels can themselves contain customer data; treat all strings as content, cap lengths, sanitize locally, and remove edit-control names by default. Password/unknown edit controls cause masking or whole-frame rejection. OCR is needed for custom canvases and missing providers, but neither UIA nor OCR proves that a screen is safe.
plan/research/07-capture-windows.md:92:**[assumption]** If hooks become necessary, first ship mouse-only click/wheel observation behind a separate toggle. The callback copies minimal data to a bounded queue, calls `CallNextHookEx`, and returns immediately; enrichment happens elsewhere. A future keyboard adapter reduces input inside the native callback to one-second activity buckets and a fixed approved shortcut vocabulary. Never export scan codes, printable virtual-key sequences, Unicode translation, exact inter-key timings, or clipboard values. Drop activity in excluded/unknown focus contexts, including during the focus-resolution race. Known shortcuts such as Copy/Paste indicate intent only, never what was copied.
plan/research/07-capture-windows.md:108:UAC consent/credential prompts use the secure desktop by default, separate from the user's ordinary desktop. UIAccess can bypass some UI privilege isolation restrictions, with signing and secure-installation requirements. These are not ordinary capture permissions. [verified: [How UAC works](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-gb/windows/security/application-security/application-control/user-account-control/how-it-works), [UIAccess policy](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/previous-versions/windows/it-pro/windows-10/security/threat-protection/security-policy-settings/user-account-control-allow-uiaccess-applications-to-prompt-for-elevation-without-using-the-secure-desktop)]
plan/research/07-capture-windows.md:110:**[assumption]** Distinguish elevated applications on the normal desktop from the secure desktop. Do not assert every elevated window's pixels are invisible to WGC; UIA access, input observation, and pixels have different constraints. Conservatively exclude elevated targets in v1 even if pixels remain obtainable. Pause and flush on lock, desktop/session switch, suspend, or inability to establish a safe foreground context. Resume only after validation. No administrator observer, SYSTEM service, UIAccess helper, UAC-policy changes, or credential-dialog automation is in scope. Windows limitations must not be presented as idle user time.
plan/research/07-capture-windows.md:118:Administrators have Recall-specific policies for feature availability, disabling snapshots, application/URI exclusions, and retention/storage. On managed devices an administrator permitting the feature does not replace individual user snapshot consent. [verified: [WindowsAI policy CSP](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-gb/windows/client-management/mdm/policy-csp-windowsai)]
plan/research/07-capture-windows.md:134:**[assumption]** Prefer Artifact Signing if the owner's legal publisher qualifies; otherwise obtain ordinary publicly trusted Authenticode signing from a suitable CA. Do not buy EV solely for SmartScreen. Sign and timestamp the observer, UI executables, helpers, and final MSIX using a stable publisher identity; verify signatures after packaging. Restrict signing to approved release jobs with short-lived CI identity. A signed outer package must not become an excuse to distribute unsigned executables that EDR evaluates separately.
plan/research/07-capture-windows.md:136:MSIX/App Installer supports configurable update behavior and enterprise deployment controls. [verified: [MSIX deployment and updates](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/msix/desktop/managing-your-msix-deployment-enterprise)]
plan/research/07-capture-windows.md:138:**[assumption]** Direct signed MSIX plus HTTPS `.appinstaller` is the initial distribution choice, with a tested previous-version recovery procedure. An EXE/MSI installer would offer flexible placement and bootstrap scripts, but would also add an updater/startup/uninstall system and would not, by itself, supply the documented OCR package identity. Sparse packaging is a later compatibility option, not a second v1 installation path. Prove packaged Rust helper activation, local IPC, Flutter/Tauri UI integration, and externally installed Muse execution in an early spike. Keep generated tools/workspaces outside the immutable package. Store submission is later and needs policy review of capabilities, generation, and external tool execution; MSIX does not imply Store approval.
