plan/research/05-alt-builders.md:53:1. `initialize` identifies the client and returns schema identity, granted capabilities, platform and sandbox availability. Absent sandbox availability means unknown, not safe.
plan/research/05-alt-builders.md:62:**[assumption]** Keep separate hosts for separate sandbox postures. The local help says sandbox and durability are fixed at host construction; approval mode is selected on the wire. Do not attempt to loosen sandboxing using arbitrary `session/start.config` keys: its currently declared member is `mcpServers`, not a general policy document. **[local: checked on machine: CLI help and `SessionConfig`]**
plan/research/05-alt-builders.md:80:**[assumption]** For a cross-platform shell, choose xterm.js plus portable-pty, joined by a tightly scoped local IPC bridge. If the product chooses native Swift UI, use SwiftTerm on Mac and accept a second renderer on Windows. Test Windows PTY behavior in Windows CI and with an external tester; a Mac PTY test cannot establish Windows correctness. Muse documents Windows support, PowerShell-based commands and possible first-time sandbox UAC prompts. **[verified: https://dev.meta.ai/docs/muse-code]**
plan/research/05-alt-builders.md:82:**[assumption]** Spawn the Muse executable directly with an argument array, controlled environment and project working directory. Handle resize, Unicode, bracketed paste, keyboard navigation, suspend/resume and terminating the entire process tree. A terminal is not a sandbox. Do not use terminal text parsing to decide that tests passed, an approval was granted, or a build may be installed. Inspect artifacts independently.
plan/research/05-alt-builders.md:84:**[assumption]** Disable terminal-driven clipboard writes and unreviewed hyperlink opening; isolate its WebView from application secrets and privileged bridge methods. Terminal output is untrusted, consistent with xterm.js's security guidance. **[verified: https://xtermjs.org/docs/guides/security/]**
plan/research/05-alt-builders.md:105:**[assumption]** Keep sandboxing on. A task requiring unavailable authority should fail into a “Needs access” state, then be continued interactively through MSP; do not silently retry with `--yolo` or treat a CLI approval-mode name as equivalent to an MSP enum. Establish headless denial behavior with the pinned build before shipping. `--output-schema` constrains the final message, not code edits or side effects.
plan/research/05-alt-builders.md:107:**[assumption]** This route is ideal for the first vertical slice and batch repair. It remains useful after MSP exists. It should not become the scheduler for ordinary saved automations: building software and executing software are different operations.
plan/research/05-alt-builders.md:126:**[assumption]** Therefore enforce limits in our loop: eight model requests, twenty tool calls, two repair attempts, output-byte ceilings and a per-build token budget. Wait for complete arguments, validate types and authority, then execute serially. Save checkpoints and safe tool errors. Plain text without a valid artifact is “needs clarification”, not success. Preserve the selected API's reasoning replay contract and recheck current schemas before implementation.
plan/research/05-alt-builders.md:128:**[assumption]** This is manageable for graph generation. It is a poor use of a solo developer's time to recreate a general coding harness, context compaction, dependency repair and shell-policy system. Delegate those jobs to Muse. Direct-loop failure can fall back to Muse generating the same graph; it must not automatically escalate to arbitrary code.
