797:Generate TypeScript with ordinary pure functions and a small SDK. Reject a bespoke graph DSL because its expression system and escape hatches would become a second programming language; reject full native scripts because they bypass the effect boundary. TypeScript is an authoring choice, not a sandbox. The initial SDK is deliberately small:
812:Initial effects are selected-CSV reads and staging one create-only CSV output, with host-rendered parameter forms. Clipboard, notifications, moves, and additional families wait. CSV/date/money/text helpers are pinned host-owned library code; decimal amounts must not silently become binary floating-point money. No eval, dynamic imports, arbitrary dependencies, subprocess, HTTP, AppleScript, PowerShell, or UI control. Add PDF/image/API recipes only as fixed app-owned operations after their own tests. SDK limits are enforced by the broker even if generated code bypasses the wrapper. `files.createOutput` records staged bytes, never grants a live write.
850:JSON Schema `format` names above are app-defined validators, not built-in filesystem authority. The host recomputes required capability union, file hashes, limits, and risk from actual artifact/plan checks. Approval signs a canonical digest excluding the signature itself; it binds source, dependencies, runtime/SDK pin, manifest, tests, and grant revision. Any change invalidates execution until reviewed. A software device signing key in the OS vault is sufficient initially; hardware-backed keys are optional, not an untested MVP dependency or malware-proof guarantee.
854:**Runtime admission comes before a general SDK.** Choose a checksum-verified Deno release and one macOS launch: a signed launcher invoking a Seatbelt wrapper with a host-owned deny-by-default profile active before module loading. This is a proposed developer spike, not demonstrated confinement. M0 must produce a runtime lock artifact containing the actual Deno version/download digest, exact argv/environment, launcher/helper signatures and entitlements, OS versions, full sandbox profile/hash, allowed code/cache/pipe paths, aggregate limit enforcement, and denial receipts. `release-runtime-1` in §7.2 is a placeholder, not a pin. No generated execution outside the synthetic developer experiment until this artifact passes. If Seatbelt compatibility/support fails, choose the fixed host-owned transform representation below immediately; do not accumulate a second Mac sandbox project in the first product.
866:Use replay for private fixtures and **stage once → review → trusted commit** for each real input. Generated code reads the selected snapshot and produces bytes into private encrypted staging, then exits and is reaped **before approval**. Preview renders those same staged bytes. The broker commits the frozen bytes without rerunning generated code and without another generated-code callback. There is no generated `live` mode. Any rerun, changed input, code/runtime/SDK, destination, limit, or grant revision needs a fresh stage and approval.
868:Define `planHash = SHA-256(canonical JSON plan)`, with host-defined field ordering/encoding and ordered effects. The plan binds source/version, runtime/SDK/dependency hashes, input snapshot digests and source identity/preconditions, resolved destination parent/volume identity and basename, ordered create effects with staged byte lengths/digests, aggregate limits, and grant/policy revision. User approval signs that digest; artifact approval and per-run effect approval are separate. Staging becomes read-only after hashing, and trusted commit rehashes the bytes. Immediately before commit, reopen/verify native handles without following symlinks, recheck identities, input preconditions, destination absence, and revocation. Use atomic no-overwrite publication of the approved bytes; if the filesystem cannot provide the required semantics, refuse that destination rather than emulate with a racy existence check. Journal intent and result; a crash between publication/receipt is reconciled by identity/digest and never blindly retried. MVP has exactly one create effect/run, one active run/tool, and two globally.
870:Adversarial acceptance includes a program that varies output by mode/time/randomness/input size: approval must commit the reviewed digest even if another execution would differ. Also test staging tamper, input/destination swaps, revocation before commit, and crash after publication. Later rename/move batches approve a frozen source→destination/identity list and ordered journal with collision checks; stop on partial failure and offer only conflict-checked recovery for completed entries. No batch enters MVP without that evidence. Timeout/denial kills the entire process tree, pauses the tool, and disables queued triggers.
878:Journal effect intent, target token, staged/committed hashes, and completion receipt. MVP preserves originals and refuses overwrite; it links the created output rather than implementing general undo. Later create undo moves output to Trash only if identity/digest still match; overwrite/move undo verifies no intervening change before restoring encrypted preimages. Retain later undo seven days. No universal undo claim for clipboard changes or external services; only expose an inverse when current preconditions hold.
880:Trust starts supervised, and each new MVP input requires staged-byte review. Later, three clean runs may offer fewer navigation prompts within the same enforced scope, never safety certification or a waiver of output approval. Future unattended folder triggers require a separate authority/effect-approval design plus standing grants and readiness/debounce/idempotency checks; context suggestions never auto-run. Any code/permission change resets trust. Three ordinary failures trip a circuit breaker; any sandbox denial stops immediately.
902:A local injection classifier may flag suspicious content later; Prompt Guard is not an MVP dependency because the plan does not require another model invocation or rely on classifier accuracy. Static scans provide diagnostics, never proof that arbitrary TypeScript cannot hide an effect.
906:All requirements apply before the named surface ships. Capture is removed from MVP, so P1/P2 and capture-specific storage/evaluation are deferred with the feature; security of shipped data and execution is not deferred.
